    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d859">

                <pb facs="0873" n="615" xml:id="pg615"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1902</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schmedeman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Christiania</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-11-17T11:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 17, 1915, 11 a. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 2.40 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>37. It has come to my knowledge that the British Government and the Norwegian
                    American Line nave made another agreement (see my despatch No. 91<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d859-f1"/>), from which it would appear that the British Government
                    waives the right of search on condition that it be put in possession of the
                    ship’s manifests and that the line agree, upon being requested by the British
                    Minister at Christiania, to return to England such goods destined for other
                    countries as the British Government may desire.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schmedeman</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
